sam.dev: Heads-up: the onboarding sample app for the edge runner is failing typechecks when you use the ESM build + our auth helper. Seems like a tsconfig/moduleResolution mismatch.

lucy: ugh, same. Error is loose: ‘Type 'unknown' is not assignable to type 'string'’ inside the auth wrapper. Works fine in Node CJS build.

khoa: can you paste the minimal repro? I suspect our overloads for createClient(auth) are collapsing to any/unknown in ESM emit.

sam.dev: repro below. Minimal sample that fails in Workers (tsconfig target=es2022, module=esnext, moduleResolution=node):

```ts
// sdk.ts (ESM build)
export function createClient(token: string): Client;
export function createClient(opts: { token?: string; refresh?: () => Promise<string> }): Client;
export function createClient(arg: any) {
  // runtime picks mode
  return new Client(arg);
}

// app.ts
import { createClient } from './sdk';
const client = createClient({ token: D1.get('TOKEN') }); // D1.get returns unknown in edge runtime types
client.request('/ping');
```

lucy: Type error: Argument of type '{ token: unknown; }' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string | { token?: string; refresh?: () => Promise<string> }'.

maria: The core issue is twofold: (1) edge ambient types (Cloudflare Durable Objects / D1) expose some APIs as unknown; (2) our overloads are not narrowing when arg contains unknown and TS 5+ in ESM mode preserves unknown differently.

khoa: also we've seen that when module=esnext + declarationMap emit, generics/overloads in .d.ts can be emitted differently by tsc, and downstream tsconfig with "moduleResolution": "bundler" or "node16" behaves oddly.

sam.dev: workaround that worked for me: cast the D1 value at import point, or add a thin runtime guard: `const token = String(D1.get('TOKEN'))` — but that's not great for onboarding.

lucy: could we change overloads to a single generic signature to avoid the overload-resolution quirk? e.g., `export function createClient<T extends string | { token?: string }>(arg: T): Client` — but that felt wrong for DX because it weakens inference.

maria: propose a smaller surface fix: export a helper type and use `as const`/narrowing helper in our samples and the auth wrapper. Also add a note in onboarding: when using edge env types that return unknown, cast or validate before passing into SDK helpers.

khoa: Another option: update our published d.ts to include an explicit overload that accepts `Record<string, unknown>` for the edge shim paths — basically `createClient(arg: any | Record<string, unknown>): Client` as the last fallback overload. It maintains stricter overloads for normal users but avoids the ESM/edge emit failure. Thoughts?

sam.dev: That's pragmatic. Less breaking than moving to a single generic. But we should add a test: build ESM .d.ts + run TS project with "moduleResolution": "bundler" and Cloudflare worker types. CI should fail if we regress.

lucy: +1 for CI test. Also add a short onboarding blurb and an example snippet for Cloudflare Workers that shows the safe pattern: `const token = typeof D1.get('TOKEN') === 'string' ? D1.get('TOKEN') : String(D1.get('TOKEN'))` or use our helper `safeString()`.

maria: I'll draft a tiny PR: (1) add fallback overload in d.ts, (2) update sample app, (3) add test using ts-node/transpile with node16/bundler resolution.

khoa: awesome. @ci-bot can you spin up a pipeline that runs that matrix (module: esnext/es2015, resolution: node,bundler,node16) against our sample app?

ci-bot: CI job queued: edge-ts-config-matrix (3 configs). Will report back. :gear:

sam.dev: thanks all — saved a new onboarding bug ticket: #DX-1241 and flagged sample app as blocked for edge-ready docs until PR merges.

lucy: small note: also check that our README examples don't show `any` casts (e.g., `as any`) — that masks the underlying issue and confuses newcomers.

maria: agreed, will avoid `any` in samples. I'll @sam.dev and @lucy when PR is ready for review.

sam.dev: cheers :rocket:
